# Buena Vida OS

Internal task app for an 11-person Costa Rican coffee company.
Next.js 15 (App Router, TypeScript, Tailwind, shadcn/ui) on Railway.
Supabase for Postgres, Auth, and Storage. Anthropic API for the assistant.
Full spec in BUENAVIDA_SPEC.md — read it before writing code.

## Security — non-negotiable

- All database access goes through supabase-js carrying the signed-in user's
  access token, using the request-scoped client in lib/supabase/server.ts.
  Never import `pg` or open a direct Postgres connection: a raw pool connects
  as the table owner and bypasses every RLS policy. `pg` is permitted in one
  file only, the migration runner.
- SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY may only be imported inside app/api/cron/**.
  If you are tempted to use the service role to make a query work, the RLS
  policy is wrong. Fix the policy.
- Access control lives in RLS policies, never in a JavaScript `if`. A UI check
  is a convenience, never the enforcement point.
- `is_admin` does not imply read access. Admin manages people and teams;
  reading is granted by team membership and by the `owner` role only.
- The `guest` role never gets team-wide read. A guest sees only tasks they own
  or are assigned, even in a team they belong to. The team branch of the read
  policy is gated on `not has_role('guest')`. Guests are also excluded from the
  brand library and the daily digest.
- File bytes never go in Postgres. No bytea, no base64 columns. Bytes go to
  Supabase Storage; only metadata rows go in the attachments table.
- Storage buckets are private. Every download is a server-minted signed URL
  with a 60s TTL. Never make a bucket public, never persist a signed URL.
- Reject SVG uploads. Validate MIME type and size server-side before minting
  an upload URL.
- Never swallow an exception. No bare `catch {}`. Log to Sentry and return
  a typed error.

## The assistant

- The model never generates confirmation text. Tool executions are written to
  the tool_calls table with a status, and the UI renders ResultCard from that
  row. Never render a success indicator from assistant prose.
- Tools never accept a UUID the model invented. A task_id must have appeared in
  a prior tool result within the same conversation; validate against that
  conversation's tool_calls rows before executing.
- team_id and user_id are derived server-side from the session and the user's
  default_team. They are never tool parameters the model fills in freely.
- Every tool returns a discriminated union:
  { ok: true, data } | { ok: false, code, detail }
  where code is not_found | forbidden | ambiguous | validation | server_error.
  Never return a string.
- Name resolution never guesses. An ambiguous assignee returns
  { ok: false, code: 'ambiguous', options: [...] }.
- Cap the tool loop at 6 iterations per turn.

## Dates

- All relative date parsing goes through lib/agent/dates.ts, which resolves in
  America/Costa_Rica (UTC-6, no DST). Never use `new Date()` for user-facing
  date logic.
- tasks.due_date is a DATE. Do not convert it to a timestamp anywhere.
- Strip trailing date phrases from task titles before insert.

## Language

- Every user-facing string lives in lib/i18n/es.ts. No Spanish literals in JSX.
- Neutral Latin American Spanish. Never vosotros, vale, or ordenador.
- Sentence case. Plain verbs. A button that says "Guardar" produces a toast
  that says "Guardado".
- Enum values stay English in the database. Translate at render time only.

## Conventions

- Server Components by default. 'use client' only where there is interactivity.
- UI mutations use Server Actions, not API routes.
- Validate every tool input and every Server Action input with zod.
- Write a task_events row in the same transaction as every task mutation.
- No `any`. Use the generated types in lib/db/types.ts; regenerate after every
  migration with `supabase gen types typescript --linked`.
- Tasks are never deleted. Use status = 'cancelled'.
- Mobile first: design at 390px, treat desktop as the adaptation.
